In .NET 10, `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` was removed because Android deprecated the underlying `adjustResize` mode. The replacement is the **edge-to-edge** layout model with `AndroidX.Core` window-insets handling.

**Quick fix — use the new `KeyboardResize` API:**

```csharp
// In MauiProgram.cs or your page handler
#if ANDROID
builder.ConfigureLifecycleEvents(events =>
{
    events.AddAndroid(android => android.OnCreate((activity, _) =>
    {
        // Ensure edge-to-edge is enabled (default in .NET 10)
        AndroidX.Core.View.WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(activity.Window, false);
    }));
});
#endif
```

Then in your layout, wrap your input bar so it responds to insets. The simplest approach:

```xml
<!-- Use a Grid with the input at the bottom -->
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ... />
    <Entry Grid.Row="1" x:Name="MessageEntry" />
</Grid>
```

And apply the keyboard inset padding via a handler:

```csharp
// In Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs or a custom handler
using AndroidX.Core.View;

ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
    Platform.CurrentActivity.Window.DecorView, 
    new InsetsListener());

class InsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        // imeInsets.Bottom gives you the keyboard height in pixels
        v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, imeInsets.Bottom);
        return insets;
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

1. `adjustResize` is gone — don't set `SoftInputMode` in the manifest.
2. .NET 10 enables edge-to-edge by default on Android 15+.
3. Use `WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime()` to detect keyboard height and adjust padding/margin accordingly.

If you want a simpler community solution, the `CommunityToolkit.Maui` package has a `KeyboardBehavior` that wraps this logic for you.
